# gorb-os — agent notes

Independent ESP-IDF firmware project (see `README.md`). This file only records
the gotchas that cost real time on the bench; it does not restate the README.

## Long-running bench commands must run detached — the 30 s tool cap is real

**Rule: anything that takes over 30 s MUST be launched in the background and
polled, never run inline.** A command that runs past ~30 s is killed by the tool
timeout *mid-flight*, which for these operations is not just an annoyance — a
killed WiFi OTA leaves a half-written inactive slot, and you pay the wait twice
(kill, then re-issue). The reliable idiom:

```
# write the work into /tmp/foo.sh, then launch fully detached and return now:
setsid /tmp/foo.sh </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 & disown
# ...the script itself appends "EXIT=$?" / "DONE $(date +%T)" to a /tmp log,
# and you poll that log with short `sleep N; cat/grep <log>` calls.
```

Two launch gotchas that burned time this session, both avoided by using a
**script file** rather than an inline one-liner:
- `nohup curl … &` alone still blocks the tool call — you must `setsid … &
  disown` AND redirect stdin/stdout/stderr (`</dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1`) for it
  to actually detach and return immediately.
- Nested double-quotes inside an inline `bash -c '…'` launcher break the shell.
  Put the payload in a file instead of quoting it around.
- Each command in a `run_commands` array is its **own shell**; a `cd` in one
  element does not carry to the next. Prefix every element with its own `cd`,
  or use absolute paths.

### Known operations that exceed 30 s (run these detached)
- **WiFi OTA push**: `curl --data-binary @build/gorb-os.bin
  http://<ip>/api/v1/ota` — ~40 s for the ~1.3 MB app over a healthy link, and
  it **aborts mid-stream** ("upload aborted by client") when rssi is marginal
  (< ~-70, ICMP loss). Prefer **serial flash** for the app on a flaky bench
  (`/tmp/serflash.sh` does app+otadata to ota_0 in ~25 s, hashes verified); use
  WiFi OTA only when the device is near its AP. The device keeps booting the old
  slot during a push, so it looks "silent" — that is the httpd worker blocked in
  the flash write, not a hang; poll, don't re-push (a second push corrupts).
- **Serial flash of a bulk image**: any `esptool write_flash` / `idf.py flash`
  that sends the app (1.3 MB) — and especially anything that re-sends
  `assets/clock_assets.bin` (~1.85 MB) — runs 25–40 s+. Detach and poll
  `/tmp/serflash.log` for `Hash of data verified` + `EXIT=0`.
- **`idf.py build` that recompiles many objects**: the steady-state rebuild of
  one file (`orb_net.c`) is ~10 s and is fine inline, but the **first build into
  a fresh `-B` dir**, or any build after an `sdkconfig`/Kconfig change that
  drags in mbedtls/LWIP, exceeds 30 s. Run detached and grep the log for
  `Project build complete` / `binary size` / `warning:` / `error:`.
- **`idf.py flash monitor`**, and any `monitor`/serial capture held open longer
  than ~25 s (e.g. watching the association ladder). Use the short bounded
  capture scripts (`/tmp/cap.py <log> <secs>`, `/tmp/cap_reset.py`) with a
  duration under ~12 s inline; longer captures go detached.

### Known operations that are SAFE inline (under 30 s)
- `python3 tools/host_tz_test.py` (~1 s).
- Steady-state `idf.py build` after editing a single source file (~10 s).
- `esptool erase_region` / a status `curl` / `ping` / `nm`+`strings` on an elf.
- Short serial captures (≤ ~12 s) like `/tmp/cap_reset.py`.

## sdkconfig is local + untracked — a NEW Kconfig `default y` does NOT appear on a rebuild

`sdkconfig` and any `sdkconfig.defaults` are NOT committed here (and there is no
`*_defconfig`). `idf.py build` treats an existing `sdkconfig` as authoritative:
adding a `config FOO` with `default y` to `main/Kconfig` does **not** materialize
`FOO=y` into the existing `sdkconfig`/`build/config/sdkconfig.h` — so
`#if CONFIG_FOO` silently compiles the feature **out**, and the only tell is a
binary that barely grows. This bit `CONFIG_ORB_NET_CAPTIVE_DNS`: the first "clean
build" was a false pass (+96 B = just the URI strings, not the task).

Therefore, **whenever you add a Kconfig symbol and want it on, prove it actually
compiled in — do not trust `EXIT=0`:**
1. grep `build/config/sdkconfig.h` for `#define CONFIG_<SYM> 1` (absent = out);
2. `xtensa-esp32s3-elf-nm build/gorb-os.elf | grep <new_fn>` and
   `strings … | grep <new log line>`;
3. sanity-check the `binary size` delta is the right order of magnitude.
Fix a missing symbol by writing the explicit `CONFIG_<SYM>=y` line into
`sdkconfig` (append if absent), then rebuild. Fresh clones get `default y` for
free; it's the *existing* local `sdkconfig` that silently excludes it.

## The config pages are C strings, and a JS syntax error in them is invisible on a radio-less bench

There are two pages, each one giant `static const char[]` of concatenated
`"…"` fragments: `HTML_LANDING` (provisioning at `/`) and `HTML_TZ` (the clock
at `/timezone`). The timezone UI lives only on the clock page — the owner took
it off the network page outright after the first phone tests. The clock page
renders city rows from `/api/v1/config` (`label` + `iana` columns), reads the
phone's zone via `Intl…resolvedOptions()` and auto-applies it; for a zone the
table does not carry it SYNTHESIZES the DST rule client-side from the phone's
own IANA database (bisection of monthly offset windows, refused when the
pattern moves between years — Jerusalem/Casablanca class), and every set is
verified by comparing the phone's offset against the device's measured
`tz_offset` echo. It shows no POSIX rules to a person, ever — the rules are
built in the browser and posted, never displayed. The zone table itself
(`s_tz_tbl`) is machine-generated from the IANA zone of the same name and the
host suite re-proves every row against the machine's tzdata (2025-2028, every
noon + the minute either side of every transition); regenerate rows via
`tools/host_tz_test.py`'s derivation, do NOT hand-edit a rule, and key new
rows on the canonical IANA name (the five `US/*` keys are frozen because NVS
may already store them). `tools/host_tz_test.py` parses and pins both pages
and enforces those boundaries by forbidding tz UI on the landing page and
`posix`/<select> on the clock page. Three ways the pages break in a
way curl + a working server can hide:

1. **Unbalanced parens across fragment boundaries.** Each C line looks fine in
   isolation; only the compiler's concatenation makes `say(a+(b?'x':'')+(c?'y':''))`
   wrong, and a phone renders the result as a page stuck at "loading…" with an
   empty network list AND an empty timezone dropdown — because the first parse
   error kills the entire `<script>`, so `status()`, `scan()` and `tzload()` all
   silently never run. That empty-dropdown symptom (reported from a real phone)
   was exactly this: one extra `)`. Reviewing source line-by-line shows nothing.
2. **A blocking handler starving the page's parallel requests.** See the
   `NET_CMD_SCAN`/`scan_collect` code: the original ran
   `esp_wifi_scan_start(block=true)` on httpd's single worker, so the status poll
   and the tz fetch queued behind a 5-10 s scan. Sequential curl never shows it;
   a browser fires its first requests at once.
3. **A call to a function whose definition was deleted.** Syntax-clean, every
   substring pin green, and the button just does nothing on the phone. This
   happened for real while writing the synthesis engine (a rewrite dropped
   `gmtRule`'s definition but left its call). The host suite lints both pages
   for calls to functions that do not exist — keep that lint green too.

`tools/host_tz_test.py` now rebuilds the page bytes exactly as the compiler does
(concatenate the fragments, C-unescape) and parses the `<script>` with esprima —
a syntax error fails the host suite. This is the file's own rule in action: what
the no-radio bench can't observe must be lifted to a host check. **Keep that check
green whenever you touch the page.** (Verified by mutation: re-injecting the `)`
turns it red.) esprima is not stdlib — a missing parser is a loud SKIP, not a
pass, so `pip install esprima` if it skips. The suite is self-verifying:
`python3 tools/host_tz_test.py --mutations` re-runs it against fourteen
deliberate regressions (deleted synth engine, rubber-stamped echo check,
hand-edited rule, shrunk buffer, …) and every one must fail it with a named
verdict; a battery that cannot fail is not a test.

## Simulating the phone side of provisioning on the radio-less bench

The host has no WiFi radio, so "a phone joins orb-xxxxxx" is the one link the
bench cannot supply — but everything AFTER the first join is testable as a
station: once a phone has provisioned the orb onto a network the bench can
also reach (the home LAN), the orb boots connected, its LAN IP answers curl,
and APSTA lets tooling open the AP (`POST /api/v1/wifi/ap {}` — the config
page has no button for this, see the h_wifi_ap comment) while the station
stays up. That exercises the full page, the connected-ack AP close (watch
serial for `SoftAP down (lease held, hold expired)`), and captive DNS.
Credentials SURVIVE app re-flashes — only `erase_region 0x9000` (nvs) +
`0xD60000` (storage) removes them — so keep one bench orb provisioned and
re-wipe only to test first-boot provisioning itself.
(Older notes mentioned `www/provisioner.html.gz` and `WIFI_SSID` baked from
`.env` at build time: that mechanism is NOT in this tree — provisioning is
entirely `HTML_LANDING` in `orb_net.c`. Verify before repeating that claim.)

## Bench has no WiFi radio — verify what you can, and say what you can't

There is no wireless adapter on the host, so the phone-side captive-portal popup
("Sign in to network") and any real client joining the AP cannot be proven here.
What IS verifiable on the bench, via USB serial + the LAN the orb joins as a
station:
- Serial console: `sudo` on `/dev/ttyACM0` (re-attach via usbipd drops dialout).
  Flash/boot capture: `/tmp/cap_reset.py`, `/tmp/cap.py <log> <secs>`.
- `/api/v1/status` over LAN (state/fw/slot/img/rssi/synced/ap/assoc_fails).
- AP open/close lifecycle + `captive DNS answering for …` via
  `/tmp/ap_cp_test.sh` (opens AP while station holds a lease, checks the station
  stays connected+synced, fires the probe endpoints, closes AP, greps serial).
- **Audit loop**: flash, then confirm `/api/v1/status`'s `fw` is the git hash you
  built (`-dirty` = uncommitted edits). Ship = test = commit, same bytes.

Anything needing a radio to *observe* (the popup, real DNS answer to a client)
belongs in `tools/host_tz_test.py` by lifting the pure logic verbatim — see how
`dns_build_reply`, `retry_delay_s`, `sta_leave_is_self_inflicted` and the tz
block are tested. Do not claim a radio-dependent behavior is verified.
## Clock themes from watch-designer kits (tourbillon/animated second hand)

`tools/bake_clock.py` takes a layered watch kit (`assets/sources/watch-orbital-kit.zip`
is the shipped example: base gear frames, hands/, layers/{dial,glass,outer}, a
15-frame tourbillon/, and a manifest.json carrying every pivot/centre — trust the
manifest, it is the renderer that made the art) and emits `assets/clock_assets.{bin,h}`
as one pair; they are generated together and must be committed together — the
firmware reads `clk_theme` by layout, so a stale half of the pair is a corrupt dial.
System python has no PIL; use a venv with `pillow numpy` for both tools.

    /tmp/bakevenv/bin/python tools/prep_watch_kit.py assets/sources/watch-orbital-kit.zip /tmp/out
    /tmp/bakevenv/bin/python tools/bake_clock.py assets/sources/orbital-tourbillon \
        --bg-frames /tmp/out/frames --bg-fps 15 \
        --tourbillon <unzipped kit>/tourbillon --tourbillon-fps 15 \
        --force-sweep --tick-rate 15 --dump-dir assets/preview-orbital

Layer mapping (kit drawOrder -> compositor slots, verified against the ink radial
surveys — check the same numbers before trusting a new kit's art into a slot):
the gear base is the `--bg-frames` delta bank; the tourbillon is the SECOND-HAND
slot as a frame bank (`--tourbillon`, max 16 frames, per-frame diff rects chained
vs predecessor exactly like the delta bank); the dial is `static1` via
`hands.order=[2,3,0,1]` (over the carriage, under the hands); the glass sheen is
the single `clock_overlay.png`; the bezel is matted INTO the bank frames by
`prep_watch_kit.py` because its rim is fully opaque and no layer above reaches
r>=220 (minute hand tops out at r=199) — a runtime ring would cost PSRAM blends
the canvas restore serves for free. Glass must stay the overlay: the kit draws
hands UNDER glass, so folding it anywhere else is a fidelity loss.

Firmware rules this dial depends on (`main/clock/clock_view.c`):
- `tourbillon_advance()` swaps the shown frame off the wall clock (15 fps) and
  marks ONLY the union of the outgoing+incoming baked diff rects at the current
  angle; the orbit itself is plain hand rotation (manifest's seconds/60x360).
  First draw marks the full swept box. Park/resume resets `s_tourb_shown`.
- The sweeping carriage's orbit step marks the ROTATED INK, not the box:
  `tourbillon_ink_mark()` (from `apply_angles`, i==2 only, AND from
  `tourbillon_advance` for the INCOMING frame at the swap angle — the baked
  diff rects are chained against a neighbour frame, and a 15 fps bank on a
  ~7 fps tick often jumps two, so neither rect covers the g->f delta; the
  incoming chord stamp is what makes the swap complete) rotates every source
  row's ink chord of frame f — per-frame chords `s_tourb_cx0/1` are measured
  once off the alpha plane into PSRAM — into the `s_wp_*` device-row spans,
  then releases <=466 `mark_rows` calls. Both orbit poses are stamped (the
  vacated pixels are the other half of the job). Fallbacks that keep the box
  union: non-L4, `s_shadow[2]`, bank never composited, chord alloc failed.
  TWO stamp invariants, both invisible to the fps counter when broken:
  1. The forward rotation uses `rot_sin_cos()` — paint_layer's exact
     interpolated /1024 trig pair — scaled **\*64** into the `>> 16` products
     (1.0 == 65536). The first builds fed LVGL's /32768 `trigo_sin` table
     (or sinma\*32) straight into the `>> 16`: stamps landed at HALF radius,
     the carriage's outer ring was never restored/repainted, and it ghosted
     a full revolution while a crisp diff-box rectangle glared next to it.
  2. A chord whose rotated segment is WIDER than tall (|dx| > rows) stamps
     its full span on every row it (+rpad) touches — the row-walk, which is
     correct and tight for steep segments, marks only a ~7 px window per row
     there and leaked the counterweight tail near the 12 pose. rpad rows
     beyond a steep segment's ends extrapolate x by step16 (that IS covered).
  Verify any change to stamps or paint_layer with
  `python3 tools/sim_tourb_mark.py`: it ports both integer pipelines against
  the baked alpha and asserts every pixel either pose paints lies inside the
  tick's marked spans (dense at cardinal poses; exit 0 = clean).
- `mark_rows(y0,y1,x0,x1,force)`: rows are handed to the display through
  `display_mark_rows_clocked`; the GIF push marks force=true (its pixels
  reached mirror+canvas without the panel ever seeing them). The commit hook
  `clock_layer_commit` then FLUSH-GATES non-force rows: it snapshots each
  marked row, restores+re-paints, and `display_commit_row(y, memcmp)` sends
  only rows whose rebuild came out different (LVGL-chunk rows always ride,
  via `s_chunk_row`). Without this the disc of a sweep re-DMAed ~200k px of
  identical pixels per tick and capped fps at 5 even after the blends were
  cheap. `s_row_snap` alloc failure = every marked row flushes (safe legacy).
- `mark_all_layers()` early-returns when `clk_theme.tourbillon_n > 0`: with
  full-coverage art (translucent dial + glass over every row) the classic
  per-tick full-box re-mark measured 5 fps; letting only the movers mark their
  own rows (apply_angles / tourbillon_advance) runs 9-10 fps. Classic themes
  (tourbillon_n == 0) keep the old discipline verbatim — do not "optimise" it
  for them without re-reading the freeze-bug note above that function.
- `paint_layer()` has an identity fast path at ang % 360 == 0 (the dial and the
  glass never rotate); `blend_px()` is the single blend implementation both
  loops share — keep it that way.
- `CLK_GIF_THROTTLE 2` does nothing on this art (tick period exceeds the GIF
  deadline, so every tick is due anyway); measured no fps change. Leave at 1.

The kit's orbit SWEEPS: bake with `--force-sweep --tick-rate 15`. tickRate must be
>= the bank fps so `hand_beat_ticks()` yields one tick per beat (tpb<=1 = true
wall-clock sampling every tick); a low tickRate RE-QUANTISES the sweep to that
many beats/s and at rate 1 is indistinguishable from ticking. Sweep costs no
extra fps now (see the rotated-ink + flush-gate rules above): both the orbit
step's marks and the DMA are confined to the carriage's actual ink, so dial
and glass only re-blend where the carriage passed. Quantising to tpb=2
(`--tick-rate 7`) measured NO higher fps (pipeline already saturated) for
its steeper steps — rate 15 is the best sweep setting. Classic tick art
keeps the same discipline either way — sweep lives in the theme, not the code.

Steam-punk is NOT lost: `git checkout assets/clock_assets.bin assets/clock_assets.h
main/CMakeLists.txt --` restores the shipped 1.85 MB pair (it rebuilds identically
from the same --bg-gif orb_bg.gif command, and /tmp/steam-punk-assets-backup holds
a copy). Swap the pair, `idf.py build`, and the full `idf.py flash` (the assets
partition write is the slow ~3 min part — detach, per above).

Verified on the bench at fw with this theme: boot logs `tourbillon: 15 frames @
15 fps on the second-hand slot, orbit about (233,233)`; sweep steady 6-7 fps
(avg commit ~145 ms, px/s ~0.8-1.0 M, no Guru) with `tools/sim_tourb_mark.py`
CLEAN — every pixel either pose paints provably lies inside the tick's marks.
(The earlier "9 fps" figures were taken while the stamps ran at half radius:
unmarked pixels were neither restored nor re-blended, i.e. a third of the
work — and of the artifacts — was simply being skipped. Correct marks cost
what they cost; speed work must shave per-px blend cost, not coverage.)
Before the rotated-ink marks + commit flush gate, sweep ran 5 fps at ~450
rows/commit.
`assets/preview-orbital/` holds the bake's own inspection
PNGs (canvas_NN, stale_NN must be black, sprites over checkerboard).
